# Architecture Rules

## Clean Architecture

- Follow Clean Architecture principles.
- Dependencies must point toward the domain layer or the framework-independent shared kernel.
- Business logic must not depend on rendering, UI, browser APIs, DOM, Phaser or any infrastructure library.
- Infrastructure communicates with the domain only through abstractions.
- External integrations must be implemented as adapters.

## Shared Kernel

- `src/helpers/` contains project-wide reusable utilities.
- Domain code may import only framework-independent, side-effect-free helpers, such as collection, randomization, and generic type utilities.
- Shared-kernel helpers must not depend on feature, application, presentation, rendering, or infrastructure modules.

## Domain

- Business logic belongs only to the domain layer.
- Domain classes must remain framework-independent.
- Domain classes must not access browser APIs directly.
- Domain classes must not manipulate the DOM.
- Domain classes must not perform rendering.
- Domain classes must not access localStorage, sessionStorage or network APIs directly.

## Rendering

- Rendering must be completely separated from business logic.
- Business logic communicates with rendering only through interfaces.
- Rendering implementation must be replaceable with another renderer or a mock implementation without modifying domain code.

## Dependency Injection

- Dependencies must be injected.
- Do not instantiate infrastructure dependencies inside business classes.
- Depend on abstractions instead of concrete implementations.

## SOLID

- Follow all SOLID principles.
- Prefer extension over modification.
- Keep every class focused on a single responsibility.
- New functionality should be implemented by extending the architecture instead of modifying unrelated classes.

## DRY

- Avoid duplicated business logic.
- Extract reusable behavior instead of copying implementations.

## OOP

- Prefer object-oriented design.
- Encapsulate behavior together with state.
- Avoid procedural orchestration of domain logic.
- Objects should collaborate through well-defined responsibilities.
